  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2024 PRX Quantum article is a large, open-access review of quantum computing for high-energy physics. The record shows no citations yet and no measured online attention so far, but it does show broad repository availability, dataset-citation tracking, and library holdings, which are consistent with a work positioned for technical reuse and classroom reference rather than immediate citation traction.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">\u201cQuantum Computing for High-Energy Physics: State of the Art and Challenges\u201d appears as a substantial review article in PRX Quantum and is published in gold open access, making it readily accessible to readers across institutions. The author list is extensive and international, with contributors from major research settings including RWTH Aachen University, reflecting the collaborative and field-wide scope implied by the topic.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article sits squarely at the intersection of quantum computing, theoretical physics, and computer science. Its concept profile points to a synthesis-oriented paper rather than a narrow technical report: the emphasis is on quantum states, entanglement, and quantum information as they relate to high-energy physics applications. That kind of framing typically serves as a reference point for researchers entering the area or mapping its open problems.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At this stage, the formal impact record is still early. OpenAlex reports zero citations, and the available scite and event-data signals also show no recorded uptake or public mention. That does not diminish the article\u2019s scholarly utility; it simply means the evidence base is presently thin and concentrated more in discoverability than in measured downstream citation or online discussion.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">What is visible is broader access and reuse infrastructure. OpenAIRE records multiple repository instances across several European countries, including open copies, which suggests the article has been deposited and surfaced in a range of institutional and disciplinary systems. DataCite also indicates dataset-citation tracking is active for the record, and WorldCat holdings point to library presence, both of which are useful signs for long-term visibility in research and teaching contexts.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, this is a highly visible, openly available review in an emerging interdisciplinary area, but one whose measurable impact is still in an early phase. The strongest current signal is not citation volume but reach: broad availability through repositories and indexing systems, combined with the article\u2019s role as a state-of-the-art synthesis for a technically specialized audience.</p>",
